{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     burger — the hamburger, parameterised by which dialog it opens.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     PARAMETERS
       sheet   id of the <dialog> to toggle. Defaults to the site sheet.

     The parameter is the point. The site bar opens the site map; the course
     bar will open the LESSON RAIL, which is a different dialog with different
     contents. One button, two destinations, no second implementation — and
     assets/js/nav.js already reads `data-ns-sheet` as an id rather than
     assuming there is only ever one sheet on the page.

     aria-expanded is the single fact the CSS (which animates the bars into an
     X via :has()) and the screen reader both read. Nothing here sets a class.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
<button type="button" class="ns-burger"
        data-ns-sheet="{{#if sheet}}{{sheet}}{{else}}site-nav{{/if}}"
        aria-controls="{{#if sheet}}{{sheet}}{{else}}site-nav{{/if}}"
        aria-expanded="false" aria-haspopup="dialog"
        aria-label="{{#if label}}{{label}}{{else}}{{t "Menu"}}{{/if}}">
    <span class="ns-burger__bar"></span><span class="ns-burger__bar"></span><span class="ns-burger__bar"></span>
</button>
